#### PRIORITY 10: In-Hospital Mobility & Deconditioning Prevention Programmes ("End PJ Paralysis")

##### Intervention 10.1: Ward-Based Structured "Get Up, Dressed, and Moving" Protocols
* **Implementation Level**: Clinical & Organizational.
* **Mechanism**: Implements a mandatory ward-based daily protocol where patients are assisted out of bed into their own clothes by 10:00 AM, eat meals sitting in chairs, and participate in minimum twice-daily supervised walking sessions, supported by healthcare assistants and volunteers.
* **Resource Requirement**: **Low Resources** (Education campaigns, patient clothing storage, ward orientation charts, mobility aids).
* **Implementation Requirements**: Nursing leadership buy-in, shift culture change, volunteer integration into acute wards.
* **Strength/Type of Evidence**: 
  * *Peer-Reviewed*: BMJ Open / Age and Ageing evaluation of the "End PJ Paralysis" campaign and mobility protocols (Oliver et al., 2018; Hartley et al., 2021).
  * *Government Source*: Chief Nursing Officer (CNO) England Deconditioning Prevention Campaign directives.
* **Feasibility & Timeframe**: Achievable **within 1–3 years**.
* **Barriers**: Risk-averse "fall prevention" culture leading to patients being kept in bed; understaffing of healthcare support workers on general medical wards.
* **Metrics/Indicators**: Percentage of patients dressed in daily clothes by midday; average length of stay; inpatient falls rate per 1,000 bed days; rate of discharge to original place of residence.
* **Categorization**: **Strong Evidence**.
* **Resource Sensitivity**: High global relevance; low financial barrier, driven by clinical culture and workforce alignment.

##### Intervention 10.2: Early Dedicated Acute Re-ablement Assistants on Medical Wards
* **Implementation Level**: Clinical & Organizational.
* **Mechanism**: Deploys specialized Re-ablement Assistants (supervised by AHPs) dedicated solely to mobilizing frail patients, assisting with activities of daily living (ADLs), and conducting cognitive re-orientation, decoupling routine patient mobilization from busy ward nursing duties.
* **Resource Requirement**: **Medium Resources** (Band 3/4 Re-ablement/Therapy Assistant post allocations).
* **Implementation Requirements**: Shared governance between Nursing and Therapy directorates.
* **Strength/Type of Evidence**: 
  * *Peer-Reviewed*: Clinical Rehabilitation trials on assistant-led mobility schemes (Crocker et al., 2020).
  * *Government Source*: National Audit of Intermediate Care (NAIC) reports.
* **Feasibility & Timeframe**: Achievable **within 1–3 years**.
* **Barriers**: Band 3/4 recruitment and retention challenges within the NHS context.
* **Metrics/Indicators**: Functional independence measure (FIM/Barthel Index) change from admission to discharge; time to first mobilization after acute stabilization.
* **Categorization**: **Strong Evidence**.
* **Resource Sensitivity**: Adaptable to systems utilizing non-registered care assistant support staff.

##### Intervention 10.3: Acute Delirium-Friendly Inpatient Ward Environmental Retrofitting
* **Implementation Level**: Organizational & Environmental.
* **Mechanism**: Retrofits acute medical wards with cognitive-friendly environmental modifications: high-contrast signage, large digital orientation clocks, circadian rhythm lighting (bright blue-enriched light by day, warm low-level light at night), non-slip contrasting flooring, and noise-reduction acoustic dampening to suppress hospital-acquired delirium.
* **Resource Requirement**: **Medium-High Resources** (Ward physical refitting capital cost, lighting architecture installation).
* **Implementation Requirements**: Facility engineering involvement, estates budget allocation, adherence to dementia/delirium architectural standards.
* **Strength/Type of Evidence**: 
  * *Peer-Reviewed*: The Lancet Psychiatry / Journal of Hospital Infection evaluations of ward architectural modifications on delirium and sleep (Inouye et al., 2019; Health Environments Research & Development Journal 2021).
  * *Government Source*: NHS England Health Building Note (HBN 08-02): Dementia-friendly health and social care environments.
* **Feasibility & Timeframe**: Achievable **within 3–5 years**.
* **Barriers**: Capital funding constraints; disruption caused by ward closures during refurbishment.
* **Metrics/Indicators**: Incidence of ward-acquired delirium; nighttime sedative administration rates; patient and carer satisfaction scores.
* **Categorization**: **Promising but Undervalued**. (Undervalued because physical environment is rarely prioritized compared to drug therapies, despite high efficacy in delirium reduction).
* **Resource Sensitivity**: Requires capital layout; highly relevant to modern hospital retrofits in high/middle income settings.

