### D. Feasibility Across Income Settings

| Priority Area | High-Income Countries (HIC - e.g., UK, EU, US) | Upper-Middle Income (UMIC) | Lower-Middle & Low Income (LMIC/LIC) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **D2A & Social Integration** | **High Feasibility**: Highly structured social care and complex integrated IT required. | **Moderate Feasibility**: Requires strong family-care integration and local municipal social health infrastructure. | **Low Feasibility**: Formal social care structures often minimal; community family-led models preferred. |
| **SDEC Expansion** | **High Feasibility**: Fully achievable with existing emergency floor restructuring. | **High Feasibility**: Excellent return on investment; avoids high inpatient bed expansion costs. | **High Feasibility**: Core clinical triage principles and same-day assessment areas require low capital layout. |
| **Front-Door CGA** | **High Feasibility**: Dependent on specialized geriatric workforce availability. | **Moderate Feasibility**: Requires dedicated training of general medical/nursing staff in basic frailty principles. | **Moderate-Low**: Severe deficit in specialist geriatric workforce; task-shifting to general community health workers needed. |
| **Virtual Wards (RPM)** | **High Feasibility**: Strong digital infrastructure, 5G, and remote technology ecosystems present. | **Moderate Feasibility**: Urban centers viable; rural areas limited by connectivity and device supply. | **Low Feasibility**: High device costs and broadband limitations; paper-based/nurse-visit home care more viable. |
| **In-Hospital Mobility ("PJ Paralysis")** | **High Feasibility**: Culture change and nursing leadership-driven. | **High Feasibility**: Highly scalable; relies on human effort, physical movement, and family involvement. | **High Feasibility**: Highly cost-effective; negligible material costs; high yield for preventing hospital harm. |

